{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_011vHSMqcawUWDoRC6Hasd68", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sagyusam\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Four_Ceremonial_Occasions\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_clothing\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doljanchi\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capping_ceremony\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Korean_traditional_festivals\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_folklore\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Ji-yoon\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kungny%C5%8F\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_girdle_of_Korea\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:11:42.515Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "Sagyusam belt tied coming-of-age ceremony Korea", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01GYRAqRFXN7AuLEcsBE13yQ", "content": [{"title": "Sagyusam", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sagyusam"}, {"title": "The Four Ceremonial Occasions", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Four_Ceremonial_Occasions"}, {"title": "List of Korean clothing", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_clothing"}, {"title": "Doljanchi", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doljanchi"}, {"title": "Capping ceremony", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capping_ceremony"}, {"title": "List of Korean traditional festivals", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Korean_traditional_festivals"}, {"title": "Korean folklore", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_folklore"}, {"title": "Park Ji-yoon", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Ji-yoon"}, {"title": "Kungnyŏ", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kungny%C5%8F"}, {"title": "Royal girdle of Korea", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_girdle_of_Korea"}]}, "The search didn't turn up anything about a belt being tied during the ceremony itself. It did cover the garment and the ceremony.\n\n- **Sagyusam:** It's a *po*, an outer robe in hanbok, that boys wore until their coming-of-age rite, *gwallye*. The name was derived from the shape; the lower end of the garment is divided into four parts.\n- **Gwallye:** It was traditional to wear a cap with a topknot on during this event. Today, the traditional Gwallye and Gyelye ceremonies have virtually disappeared, and are seldom performed.\n- **Belts:** The only belt mentioned in the results is the *gakdae*, which is a belt worn by officials. That's a separate item from the sagyusam, and the results don't link it to the gwallye.\n\nIf you need the belt detail, a Korean-language source on gwallye procedure (for example, the Korean Wikipedia article on 관례 or a Korean cultural heritage site) would be the place to check."], "durationSeconds": 4.873704792000001, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_011vHSMqcawUWDoRC6Hasd68", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
